rmalized": false, "mention": "Saa1", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9731063842773438, "span": {"begin": 1846, "end": 1850}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:6289"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "Saa2", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9892192482948303, "span": {"begin": 1852, "end": 1856}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:6290"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "Saa3", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9867936968803406, "span": {"begin": 1861, "end": 1865}}, {"id": ["CUI-less"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "lung", "obj": "cell_type", "prob": 0.4475162923336029, "span": {"begin": 1887, "end": 1891}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:6290"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "Saa3 isoform", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.992114782333374, "span": {"begin": 1938, "end": 1950}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:351"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "APP gene", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9938639998435974, "span": {"begin": 1988, "end": 1996}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:6288"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "Saa1 gene", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9915863871574402, "span": {"begin": 2063, "end": 2072}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:6288", "6289"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "Saa isoform", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9912130236625671, "span": {"begin": 2100, "end": 2111}}], "text": "Acute phase proteins (APPs) are proteins that have an increase in plasma concentration of at least 25% during an acute phase response (Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999; Mantovani &amp; Garlanda, 2023). In humans, the major APPs are C-reactive protein and serum amyloid A, while in mice the major APPs are serum amyloid A, haptoglobin and serum amyloid P (Cray, Zaias, &amp; Altman, 2009; Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999). All animals--from fish to mammals--have APP, but the type of major APP differs by species (Cray, 2012).  It is widely accepted that APPs are mainly produced in the liver, while several other tissues have been shown to express APPs. In humans, APP genes have been shown to be expressed in the adrenal gland, adipose tissue, appendix, gall bladder, heart, kidney, liver, lung, placenta, prostate, salivary gland, skin, small intestine, stomach, thymus, thyroid, trachea and uterus (de Dios et al., 2018; NCBI, 2023; Schr&ouml;dl et al., 2016; Urieli-Shoval, Cohen, Eisenberg, &amp; Matzner, 1998; Venteclef, Jakobsson, Steffensen, &amp; Treuter, 2011). In mice, APPs have been shown to be expressed in the adrenal gland, bladder, central nervous system, colon, duodenum, genital fat pad, heart, kidney, large intestine, limbs, liver, lung, mammary gland, ovary, placenta, small intestine, spleen, subcutaneous fat pad, testis and thymus (Kalmovarin et al., 1991; NCBI, 2023; Saber et al., 2013).  Table 1 presents a list of acute phase response genes in humans and mice according the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI): Table 1.  It is important to note that humans and mice express four SAA isoforms (Saa1, Saa2, Saa3 and Saa4), while Saa3 is a pseudogene in humans (Shridas &amp; Tannock, 2019). CRP is expressed in humans and mice, although only moderately expressed in mice (Pepys &amp; Hirschfield, 2003).  In mice, Saa1, Saa2 and Saa3 are all expressed in lung tissue (Saber et al., 2014). Furthermore, the Saa3 isoform is the most differentially expressed APP gene in lung tissue and it is not highly expressed in the liver, while Saa1 gene is the most differentially Saa isoform expressed in liver tissue after exposure to particles (Halappanavar et al., 2011; Poulsen et al., 2017; Saber et al., 2014).", "timestamp": "Tue Oct 13 15:40:20 +0000 2026"}